MKNOD(8)                       P32W Unix                        MKNOD(8)

NAME
       mknod - make a special file

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/mknod name b|c major minor

DESCRIPTION
       mknod makes name a special file: with b a block device, with
       c a character device. major and minor (each 0-255) make the
       device number major * 256 + minor, which selects the driver
       and the unit; the devices of P32W Unix are listed in
       mknod(2). The mode is 0666 less the file creation mask
       (umask in sh(1)); use chmod(1) and chown(1) afterwards for
       anything else.

       Only root may make special files (otherwise: mknod: name:
       not owner (only root may make special files)). A name that
       exists already is refused (mknod: name: file exists).

EXAMPLES
       /etc/mknod /dev/null c 2 0     the null device again
       /etc/mknod /dev/ram b 1 0      the RAM disk
       chmod 600 /dev/ram

EXIT STATUS
       0 when the file is made; 1 if it exists or cannot be made;
       2 for a usage error, a bad major or minor number, or p.

SEE ALSO
       mknod(2), chmod(1), chown(1), mount(8)

BUGS
       There are no named pipes: mknod name p says
       mknod: named pipes are not supported. A device number that
       names no device is accepted; opening the file then fails
       with ENXIO.
